course: biology
lesson: 198
title: Cats as hunters: presents on the doormat and why Tom never catches Jerry
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Even a fat, sleepy pet cat is a hunter inside. Watch it with a toy mouse: it crouches, its bottom wiggles, it goes perfectly still, then it pounces and lands exactly on target.
explain: In Tom and Jerry, Tom crashes around the house after Jerry and never wins. Real cats hunt the other way round: they wait silently, creep closer and strike in one quick jump, and they often do catch mice.
explain: That is why people kept cats on farms and ships for thousands of years: to catch the mice and rats that ate the grain.
explain: When your cat leaves a mouse on the doormat it is not saying thank you or teaching you; it is simply bringing food home, the way its hunting instinct tells it to.
fact: Many cats bring prey home and leave it on the doormat or floor; the cat is carrying food back to a safe place, not thanking or rewarding you.
fact: A cat may bring in a live mouse and let it go, patting and chasing it; this looks cruel, but the cat is acting on instinct and does not understand cruelty.
fact: Real cats often catch mice, and people kept cats on farms and ships for thousands of years for this job; the Tom and Jerry story where the mouse always wins is cartoon fun.
fact: Cats are ambush hunters: they wait very still for a long time, creep close, and pounce in one short burst, rather than chasing prey wildly around a house.
fact: Sitting still and watching a mouse hole, like Tom does, is the true part of the cartoon; cats can hear the high squeaks of mice that are too high for us to hear.
fact: A real animal hit with a frying pan or squashed flat would be badly hurt; bodies cannot be flattened and pop back into shape the way cartoon animators draw them.
fact: Cats sometimes make a fast chattering or clicking sound at birds through a window; nobody knows for sure why, and ideas include frustration, a practice killing bite or copying bird calls.
fact: Pet cats kill very large numbers of small birds and animals each year, and a bell on the collar, a bright collar cover or keeping the cat in at dawn and dusk can help protect them.
fact: Cats judge distance very well and notice moving things much more easily than still ones, so they can land a pounce exactly on a moving toy.
fact: Cats chase a laser dot because it moves like prey, but it can never be caught, which can leave the cat frustrated, so play should end with a real toy the cat can grab.
fact: Cats walk almost silently thanks to soft paw pads and by walking on their toes, and they place their back paws almost exactly where their front paws stepped.
exercise: A cat leaves a dead mouse on the doormat. What is it doing? | Bringing food home, following its hunting instinct | Saying thank you for the dinner you gave it last night, like a present | Teaching you to hunt for sure | Giving the mouse a funeral
exercise: A cat brings in a live mouse and lets it go. Why? | It is acting on hunting instinct, not trying to be cruel | It wants a new pet to play with when the family is out all day | It is scared of the mouse | It wants you to cook it
exercise: In the cartoon Tom never catches Jerry. What is true of real cats? | Real cats often catch mice | Real cats can never catch mice | Real mice always beat cats | Real cats are scared of mice
exercise: Why did people keep cats on farms and ships for thousands of years? | To catch the mice and rats that ate food stores | To keep the sailors warm at night in the cold cabins below deck | To scare away seagulls | To pull small carts
exercise: How do real cats usually hunt? | They wait still, creep close and pounce | They run wildly round the house | They chase prey for hours | They bark to scare prey out
exercise: Which part of Tom's hunting is true to life? | Sitting very still watching a mouse hole | Running straight through walls and leaving a cat-shaped hole | Using a frying pan | Setting up traps with rope
exercise: Why can a cat find a mouse behind the skirting board? | It hears the mouse's high squeaks that we cannot hear | It can see right through walls with its special night eyes | It smells cheese on the mouse | It reads the mouse's tracks on the ceiling
exercise: Jerry hits Tom with a frying pan and Tom is flattened and pops back. What would really happen to a cat? | It would be badly hurt | It would pop back too | It would bounce like a ball | It would feel nothing
exercise: Why do cartoon bodies squash and stretch? | Animators draw them like that because it looks lively and funny | Cartoon cats have no bones because real cats have none either, only soft rubbery fur | Real cats can do it too when young | Cats are made of rubber
exercise: A cat chatters its teeth at a bird through the window. What do we know about why? | Nobody knows for sure; scientists have several ideas | It is cold | It is laughing at the bird because the bird cannot get through the glass | It has toothache
exercise: How can a cat owner help protect garden birds? | Put a bell or bright collar cover on the cat | Feed the cat to the birds | Let the cat out only at dawn, when all the birds are fast asleep in their nests | Paint the birds
exercise: Do pet cats kill many birds and small animals? | Yes, very large numbers each year | No, pet cats never hunt | Only cats without owners hunt | Only big cats like lions do
exercise: How does a cat land its pounce exactly on a toy mouse? | It judges distance very well | It closes its eyes and guesses | It uses its tail to aim like a dart | It smells exactly where the toy is
exercise: What does a cat notice most easily? | Things that move | Things that stand still | Things that are red | Things that are far away and still
exercise: A cat chases a laser dot on the wall. How should the game end? | With a real toy the cat can catch | By shining the laser in its eyes | By hiding the dot forever without a toy | By shouting at the cat
exercise: How does a cat walk almost silently on a wooden floor? | Soft paw pads and walking on its toes | It floats a little above the floor, like a ghost in a story | It holds its breath | It takes its claws off
exercise: Where does a cat put its back paws when it walks? | Almost exactly where its front paws stepped | Far out to the sides, to keep its balance on narrow walls | In front of its front paws | Nowhere, it hops
test: «Tom never gets Jerry so real cats are rubbish at catching mice» What is right? | No, real cats often catch mice; the cartoon is just for fun | Yes, mice always win | Yes, cats only eat cat food from a tin and have forgotten how to hunt | No, real cats catch only rats
test: «eww Smudge left a dead mouse on the mat, she's saying thank you innit» What is closer to the truth? | She is bringing food home on instinct | Yes, it is a thank-you present | She is punishing you | She wants you to bury it
test: In the cartoon Tom races through every room after Jerry. How would a real cat hunt the mouse? | Wait silently near the mouse's path and pounce in one jump | Race through every room, knocking over lamps and vases | Chase it all day without stopping, until the mouse is too tired to run | Call other cats to help
test: In the cartoon Tom gets flattened by a frying pan and is fine a second later. What would a vet say about a real cat? | It would be badly hurt and need help at once | It would be fine after a nap | It would bounce back like rubber | Cats have no bones so it is safe
test: What would happen if you played laser games with your cat and never let it catch anything? | The cat could get frustrated because it never makes a catch | The cat would learn to fly | The cat would get bored of all toys forever and never play with anything again | The cat would catch the light
test: «why's the cat making that weird clicky noise at the pigeons» What is right? | Cats often chatter at birds, and scientists are not sure why | It is choking | It is talking pigeon, which cats learn from the birds in the garden | It is cold
test: «our cat keeps bringing in baby birds, what can we do» Which is a helpful step? | A bell or bright collar cover, and keeping the cat in at dawn and dusk | Feed the birds to the cat | Shave the cat | Nothing, cats never harm birds, they only watch them from the grass all day
test: «how did he sneak up behind me, didn't hear a thing» Why? | Soft paw pads and careful steps make cats almost silent | He was floating | You were deaf for a second | Cats have tiny soft wheels under their paws that roll without a sound
test: Count them: Tom chases Jerry and two other mice. How many tails are running? | 4 | 3 | 2 | 6
test: Count them: two cats watch three mice. How many legs in the room? | 20 | 12 | 10 | 16
test: «a mouse squeaks behind the wall and I can't hear nothing» How does the cat know it's there? | Cats hear high squeaks too high for us | The mouse waved | The cat can see through walls | The cat guessed
test: A cat sits as still as a statue by a mouse hole for ten minutes, then pounces. Is this like the cartoon or like real life? | Both, this part of the cartoon is true | Only the cartoon, real cats never wait | Neither, cats do not hunt mice | Only real life, Tom never waits
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hunting method of the domestic cat? | It waits motionless, approaches by stealth and strikes in a single short burst | It pursues its prey continuously throughout the dwelling until the prey is exhausted | It relies chiefly upon barking to flush out prey | It hunts exclusively by daylight in open fields
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a domestic cat deposits dead prey upon the doormat. | It carries food to a place of safety by instinct | It expresses gratitude to its owner | It intends to punish the household | It seeks to teach the owner to read
exam: It is given that a cartoon cat is struck with a frying pan, flattened, and immediately restored. Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a living cat. | A living cat so struck would sustain serious injury | A living cat would likewise be restored at once | A living cat possesses no bones and is therefore unharmed | A living cat would merely require sleep
exam: Ascertain which of the following measures is recognised as reducing the predation of garden birds by pet cats. | Fitting a bell or brightly coloured collar cover | Feeding the cat exclusively upon milk | Permitting the cat outdoors solely at dawn | Painting the birds in bright colours
